ionale": "The baseline (-4.5) leans on a thin, partly Democratic-sponsored polling average showing a close race, but the fundamentals (-6.4), unanimous expert Likely R ratings, Ricketts's money edge, and the damaging leaked-audio story in the race's final stretch all point toward the incumbent. I shift to about R+7, consistent with markets pricing Osborn near 33%.", "watch": "Whether the leaked-audio controversy fades or further polls after mid-September confirm a genuine single-digit race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Pete Ricketts (R) is running.", "Dan Osborn is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Nebraska is a strongly Republican state, and incumbent Pete Ricketts enters the general election with a large fundraising advantage, unified party support, and a fresh $1.4 million super PAC buy on his side. Independent Dan Osborn has raised impressively and kept early polling close, but he carries the burden of an unconventional candidacy in a state that rarely elects non-Republicans to the Senate.\n\nThe race tightened in mid-September when leaked recordings surfaced of Osborn making profane remarks about some Trump supporters, which he partially acknowledged and apologized for. That controversy, arriving late, gives Ricketts a potent line of attack. Polling averages show a near-tie, but expert ratings uniformly call the race Likely R and markets favor Ricketts by roughly two to one. The evidence points to a Ricketts win with a meaningful but not overwhelming margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -4.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1133, "margin": -7.0}]}